
;;
	a branch construct is written as:
	if ops0 { ops1 }
	(elif ops0 { ops1 })*
	(else { ops1 })?

	ops0 must produce an extra element on the stack
	depending on whether it is non-zero or zero the
	following body is executed or skipped

	if may be followed by any number of elif branches,
	each with its own condition, evaluated in order until
	one succeeds
	else is optional and runs when no condition succeeded

	when else is absent, every branch body must leave
	the stack exactly as it was before the branch, since
	control may skip all of them

	when else is present, the branch bodies are free to
	change the stack, but every branch must leave it in
	the same shape, so the code after the branch sees a
	consistent result regardless of which path ran

	branches may be nested
;;

main:(){
    10 20 if 1 { >< } // dmp ; 10
    if 0 { 35 34+ }	elif 1 { foo# } else { 430 10- } dmp ; 420
    if 1 { 1 if 0 { 2 } else { 3 } } else { 4 5  } + dmp ; 4
    0 while *> 10 < { 1+ if 1 { 2+ 2- } } dmp ; 10
}
foo:(:s4){ 42 }